{
  "teamId": 120,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Washington Nationals entered the season with cautious optimism, a young roster built around electrifying talent like CJ Abrams, James Wood, and Daylen Lile. April offered a jarring preview of the season's dual nature: Cole Henry surrendered a damaging two-run single on Opening Day, yet James Wood answered days later with a three-run blast that captured the imagination of Nationals Park. The early weeks were a genuine tug-of-war, with the bullpen leaking runs in crucial spots and the offense responding just enough to keep Washington afloat near .500.\n\nMay and early June represented the season's brightest stretch. Abrams was electric, hitting a go-ahead homer on April 30th and following it with a stunning two-RBI triple in late May. Lile was blossoming into a legitimate middle-of-the-order threat, and the offense posted an .744 OPS that ranked among the better units in the National League. But the pitching staff was already showing cracks that would prove fatal, and June arrived with a brutal reckoning. Mitchell Parker was victimized for a Bryce Eldridge grand slam in one of the season's most punishing moments, and a catastrophic June 23rd against Philadelphia — featuring multiple home runs surrendered by Brad Lord — sent the Nationals into a tailspin that erased most of their first-half gains.\n\nJuly brought no relief. Richard Lovelady's disastrous midsummer outing, yielding a devastating two-run homer that swung win probability by nearly .75, encapsulated a bullpen that simply could not protect leads. Matt Krook and Clayton Beeter continued to struggle in high-leverage situations, and the Yankees and others feasted on Washington's relief corps during a stretch that effectively ended any realistic playoff hopes. The Nationals were a .500 team in talent but a sub-.500 team in execution, and the gap between potential and performance grew wider with each blown save.\n\nAugust and September became a showcase for what this team could eventually become while also serving as a painful ledger of missed opportunities. Brady House hit a three-run blast to remind scouts why he was so coveted. Lile continued to make clutch contact. But Tom Cosgrove surrendered a grand slam in early August, Will Dion was torched by Mookie Betts for a three-run shot in September, and Jake Irvin closed the year by giving up a three-run home run that summarized a pitching staff with a 4.67 ERA. Washington finished 77-85, a team with 210 home runs and genuine offensive pop, undone by a rotation and bullpen that simply gave too much back. The foundation of youth is real, but 2026 was ultimately a year of learning hard lessons.",
